  "query_preview": "..\n\nMain forecast concerns are on strong west/northwesterly winds this \nevening into the overnight hours across much of the area with \nwind concerns shifting into eastern Montana and north-central \nWyoming on Wednesday. \n\nA cold front pushes into the area tonight into Wednesday as low \npressure deepens over the Upper Midwest. An anomalously strong jet \nstreak for this time of year moves over the region late this \nafternoon through tonight as 700 mb winds increase into the 40 to \n50 kt range. South central Montana, including areas west of a \nBillings to Red Lodge line, have a 40 to 80 percent chance of \nexperiencing wind gusts over 45 mph. Winds will be even stronger \nin western areas such as Big Timber, Livingston, and Harlowton, \nincluding the Crazy Mountains, where gusts to around 50 mph can be\nexpected, with gusts over 60 mph possible in the higher \nelevations of the Crazy Mountains. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected over north-central and \nnortheast Montana late this afternoon into tonight as forcing \nincreases on the nose of the jet. CAPE and shear profiles will \nfavor a few strong to perhaps severe storms with damaging winds\nand large hail. The thunderstorms may edge into the forecast area\nlate this evening with the potential for strong to severe wind \ngusts at locations such as Miles City and Baker. \n\nNorthwest flow will be in place across the region Wednesday into \nThursday with some very low chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nmost areas aren't expected to see this activity.  Looking further \nahead into this weekend, a more active weather pattern looks to set \nup across the area as troughing moves across the Pacific Northwest \nand progresses eastward into the Northern Rockies. Several ECMWF \nEnsemble Members are showing measurable rainfall and some signals\nfor instability/MUCAPE. Continue to keep a close eye on the \nforecast for this weekend, especially later Saturday into Sunday.",
